# 🌐 DNS Troubleshooting

## Problem Identified

Internal Active Directory DNS resolution was working:

```cmd
nslookup kpatel.test
```

Result:

```text
SUCCESS
```

However, external hostname resolution was failing:

```cmd
ping google.com
```

The system reported that the host could not be found.

This indicated that DNS was partially working rather than completely failing.

---

# 🔎 Troubleshooting Approach

The issue was isolated by testing internal and external DNS separately.

## Test 1 — Internal AD DNS

```cmd
nslookup kpatel.test
```

Result:

```text
Successful
```

This confirmed that the Active Directory DNS zone was available.

---

## Test 2 — External DNS

```cmd
nslookup google.com
```

Result:

```text
External hostname could not be resolved
```

The result suggested that the internal DNS server did not have a working forwarding path for external DNS requests.

---

# 🧠 DNS Diagnosis

The results showed:

```text
Internal DNS
kpatel.test
     ↓
     ✅
```

while:

```text
External DNS
google.com
     ↓
     ❌
```

Therefore, the problem was not that Active Directory DNS was completely unavailable.

The issue was related to **external DNS resolution/forwarding**.

---

# 🔧 DNS Forwarder Configuration

DNS Manager was opened on SERVER01:

```text
Server Manager
→ Tools
→ DNS
```

Then:

```text
SERVER01
→ Properties
→ Forwarders
```

External DNS forwarders were configured.

Example:

```text
8.8.8.8
1.1.1.1
```

The purpose of the forwarders is to allow the internal DNS server to forward DNS queries for names that it does not host locally.

---

# 🌍 DNS Forwarding Architecture

The final concept is:

```text
Client asks:
"Where is google.com?"
        |
        ↓
SERVER01 DNS
        |
        | Internal name?
        | Yes → Resolve internally
        |
        | External name?
        | Yes
        ↓
DNS Forwarder
        |
        ↓
External DNS
        |
        ↓
Internet
```

For an internal domain:

```text
kpatel.test
      |
      ↓
SERVER01 DNS
      |
      ↓
Active Directory DNS Zone
```

